$DIR/main.p:37:10: cannot pass the function literal to conv, which keeps it after returning
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it refers to
    those variables where they live, in f's frame (x is declared at main.p:36).
    conv passes g to call (main.p:32), which passes s to s.String (which may
    call main.(HF).String) (main.p:11), which stores h in package variable keepF
    (main.p:27), so the function literal would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change conv to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from
    new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
